{"step_index":491,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-16T19:01:13Z","input_tokens":3913,"cache_read_tokens":93010,"output_tokens":746,"thinking":"Sampling `/proc/stat` and `/proc/meminfo`, along with ZFS ARC statistics, every 100ms within a dedicated Python subprocess appears the optimal method. This approach allows the tracking of average and peak CPU utilization (total and I/O wait) in a granular manner. This should provide sufficient data for analysis.\n\n\nThe `*_results.csv` should contain summary timing and resource usage data at a scenario level (M, N). Considering also exporting `*_step_resources.csv` to provide a granular breakdown of resource consumption per step within each scenario. The trade-off is the extra file to manage and its potential redundancy vs. the ease of detailed analysis.\n\nAvg & Peak RAM MB:\n- prov_ram_peak_mb 100\n- prov_net_ram_delta_mb 50\n- deploy_ram_peak_mb 200\n- deploy_net_ram_delta_mb 100\n- verify_setup_ram_peak_mb 150\n- verify_exec_ram_peak_mb 250\n- verify_exec_net_ram_delta_mb 125\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"ask_question","args":{"questions":"[{\"is_multi_select\":false,\"options\":[\"(Recommended) Export all three: (1) add per-step CPU/RAM columns to *_results.csv, (2) export a clean long-format *_step_resources.csv (1 row per scenario × step, ideal for bar/line plots), and (3) export the raw 100ms *_timeseries.csv for continuous timeline plots\",\"Export (1) per-step CPU/RAM columns in *_results.csv and (2) the long-format *_step_resources.csv, without the raw 100ms timeseries CSV\",\"Put all CPU/RAM columns only inside the existing *_results.csv and *_raw_samples.csv files (no extra CSV files)\"],\"question\":\"4. How would you like the CPU and RAM data structured across the output CSV files for plotting in your thesis?\"}]","toolAction":"\"Asking Question 4 on CSV output format for CPU and RAM data\"","toolSummary":"\"Ask Question 4: CSV output structure for CPU/RAM metrics\""}}]}
